30,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"It appears that projects with broad, cross-departmental accessibility are the most successful. However, I've found that work in the older blocks is frequently delayed by hazardous materials hidden in the partitions.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings show a stark imbalance; specialist rooms often sit idle, while general-purpose rooms are overbooked year-round. Furthermore, taking rooms out of service has forced us into expensive off-site rentals to maintain the timetable.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My research suggests a pattern of optimism. Projects driven by ""comfort"" or ""appeal"" tend to slip by a year or more, while those addressing inspection findings are far more punctual. Also, weather has cost us six weeks on exposed work.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"I've found some worrying gaps! Mechanical and plant costs are jumping by twenty percent, and we have no renewal reserve for our AV kit. We're planning for costs that just aren't real anymore!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"It seems we have a classic case of mismanagement. Our grounds staff are nonexistent, yet our bench technicians are wasting hours on manual window-opening routines because the equipment is obsolete. We are paying people to be human actuators.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Victoria, your data on overbooked general rooms aligns with my finding that shared accessibility leads to the highest satisfaction. We should prioritize versatility over specialization.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Mizuki, I agree. The demand for general space is constant. We must also consider the operational cost of construction; we cannot afford to disrupt the timetable and pay for off-site rentals again.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, if costs are jumping by twenty percent, I imagine our ""optimistic"" schedules are only the beginning of our troubles. We should avoid any project that risks both a budget blowout and a calendar slip.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Benjamin, exactly! It's scary. If we pick something with heavy mechanical work, we're basically asking for a budget disaster. We need a plan that's honest about the costs!","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, honesty is a rare commodity here. But let's be practical: if we ignore the fact that our technicians are performing manual labor for machines that should work, we're just planning for a different kind of disaster.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shunichi, I understand the frustration of the technicians, but we must be careful. If we prioritize a fix that requires opening walls in the older blocks, we risk the hazardous material delays I discovered.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Shunichi, while the technicians' burden is significant, we must ensure the solution doesn't create a new crisis. If a project requires taking critical rooms out of service, the cost of off-site rentals will be prohibitive.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, you've hit on a vital point. Between hazardous materials and the weather's whims, any project involving structural ""surprises"" or outdoor exposure is essentially a gamble with our calendar. I prefer a predictable path.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Shunichi, I really want to help the technicians, but @Benjamin is right! If we pick something that costs way more than the paper says, we might run out of money before the job is even finished. That would be the worst!","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Shunichi"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, your fear of running out of money is charming, but we are already paying a price in inefficiency. A predictable path that ignores the staff's burnout is just a slow-motion failure. We need a solution that is both fiscally sane and operationally lean.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"We must find a balance. A project that provides shared utility, avoids the older blocks' structural risks, and doesn't rely on expensive mechanical upgrades would be the most logical and compassionate choice for everyone.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree. We need a project that maximizes usage without triggering the hidden costs of off-site rentals or structural delays. A versatile, low-impact conversion seems the most rational way to satisfy these constraints.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are all dancing around the same conclusion. To avoid a calendar catastrophe, we must shun ""appeal"" projects and outdoor work. A low-impact, internal conversion is the only way to keep our sanity and our schedule.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm with you all! Let's find something that doesn't need fancy new machinery or expensive plant work. If we keep it simple and honest with the budget, we can actually get it done!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I'll play along with this quest for ""simplicity."" As long as we aren't just rearranging the deck chairs on a sinking ship while the technicians continue to act as manual levers, I can support a low-impact approach.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over""}]}"
50,2,4,pass,Shunichi,,Over,"{""forced"":false,""empty"":false,""trailing"":true}"
